      # cost. Pass `options.sms_sender_name` to sign one code with another name, or
      # `options.use_white_label_customization: true` to fall back to your white-label public name when no sender name
      # is set. WhatsApp codes use a fixed message; connect your own WhatsApp Business number in the Business Console
      # to send them from your business name and number, billed at the Didit fee only.
      #
      # **Send statuses.** `Success` - OTP sent for a new verification; `Retry` - OTP re-sent for the pending
      # verification; `Blocked` - the anti-fraud layer refused to send (see `reason`, e.g. `repeated_attempts`,
      # `suspicious`, `spam`). A `Blocked` send immediately finalizes the verification as `Declined` with a high-risk
      # warning, so a follow-up check returns `Expired or Not Found`. A sandbox test number registered as `blocked` is
      # the exception: its send answers `Blocked` and its check answers `Declined`.
      #
      # **Billing.** The price depends on the destination country and the channel that actually delivers the message -
      # see [Phone Verification Pricing](/getting-started/phone-verification-pricing). A `Blocked` send is never
      # charged, and neither is a send whose every message was reported undeliverable. Any other send is charged once,
      # even if its delivery was never confirmed: when its delivery report arrives, or else when the verification
      # finalizes or its code expires. Checks are always free. As an anti-abuse measure, phone verification is
      # disabled until your organization has a paid plan or makes its first top-up (`403`).
      #
      # **Session persistence.** Every new verification is persisted as an API-type session: `request_id` is a real
      # session id you can pass to `GET /v3/session/{sessionId}/decision/`, the verification appears in the Business
      # Console, and `status.updated` webhooks fire as it progresses.
      #
      # **Sandbox.** A sandbox application's send takes the same path as a live one: the same validation,
      # `on_conflict` handling, channel policy and refusals (an unsupported destination included) and the same answer,
      # with a `verification_id` you can read, cancel or approve. Only the delivery is simulated: no message is sent
      # and nothing is charged. A number you registered with `POST /v3/phone/sandbox/numbers/` plays the outcome it
      # was registered with: `blocked` answers `Blocked`, every other outcome answers `Success`, and the outcomes that
      # end the verification at send time (`not_delivered`, `silent_match`, `silent_mismatch`) settle it. For any
      # other number, check with `123456`, or with the `code` your send set.
      #
      # **Authentication.** Send your application's API key in the `x-api-key` header. A missing `x-api-key` header,
      # or a key that is scoped and does not carry write access to `sessions`, returns `403`; a wrong, revoked or
      # expired key returns `401`.
      #
      # **Rate limits.** Shared write budget of 300 requests/min per API key across all POST/PATCH/DELETE endpoints,
      # plus an anti-abuse cap of 4 send attempts per phone number per hour; exceeding either returns `429`.
      #
      # +POST /v3/phone/send/+
      #
      # @param phone_number [String] Recipient phone number in E.164 format - leading `+` and country code (e.g.,
      #   `+14155552671`). Numbers are validated and normalized to E.164; unparseable or invalid numbers return `400`
      #   with a `phone_number` field error.
      # @param options [Hash] OTP delivery options. All fields are optional.
      # @param signals [Hash] Optional device and network signals about the end user, forwarded to the anti-fraud
      #   layer to improve detection of abusive or automated traffic. All fields are optional.
      # @param vendor_data [String] Optional caller-controlled identifier (your internal user id, an email, a UUID,
      #   etc.) persisted on the session and echoed back in the send response, the matching check response, webhooks,
      #   and the Business Console. Use it to correlate Didit's `request_id` with your user record.
      # @param metadata [Hash, nil] Optional free-form JSON object persisted on the session and echoed back in the
      #   send response, the matching check response, webhooks, and the Business Console.
      # @param code [String] Supply 4 to 10 letters and digits, such as K7P2QX. Letters match in either case. Your
      #   code takes precedence over `code_length` and `options.code_size`. Choose a channel that supports
      #   caller-supplied codes, such as WhatsApp. If the allowed channels cannot carry your code, the send returns `400
      #   custom_code_unavailable`.
      # @param code_length [Integer] 4 to 10 digits, for this request. Wins over `options.code_size` when both are
      #   sent; ignored when `code` is sent.
      # @param on_conflict [String] A code is still valid: `replace` (default) sends a new one, `reuse` returns it
      #   without sending, `retry` sends the same code again in the same window (up to two retries, three sends total),
      #   `reject` answers `409 verification_in_progress`. For `reuse` and `retry`, if the pending verification does not
      #   meet the requested channel or challenge policy, the send starts a new verification. A retry also st...
      # @param trusted_user [Boolean] Your server vouches for this user: this one request goes past your fraud rules
      #   (velocity limits, risk score, network blocks, policies). Needs a `fraud_override` signed for it; a block list
      #   still stops the number.
      # @param force_challenge [Boolean] Your server asks for a challenge on this request: Didit uses only
      #   fraud-resistant channels (`fraud.channels_allowed`) and records it as challenged by you; it is not reported or
      #   scored as fraud.
      # @param fraud_override [String] A token signed for this phone number and expiry, in the form
      #   `v1.<expires_at>.<signature>`. `<expires_at>` is the Unix time in seconds, at most 5 minutes ahead.
      #   `<signature>` is the base64url encoding, without padding, of the HMAC-SHA256 of the UTF-8 string
      #   `v1\n<phone_number>\n<expires_at>` (the number in E.164 format, with a newline between each value), keyed with
      #   your application's webhook sig...
      # @param idempotency_key [String] sent as the Idempotency-Key header
      # @param request_options [Hash, nil] :timeout, :max_retries, :extra_headers, :extra_query, :extra_body
      # @return [Didit::Types::PhoneVerificationSendResponse]
      def send_code(
        phone_number:,
        options: OMIT,
        signals: OMIT,
        vendor_data: OMIT,
        metadata: OMIT,
        code: OMIT,
        code_length: OMIT,
        on_conflict: OMIT,
        trusted_user: OMIT,
        force_challenge: OMIT,
        fraud_override: OMIT,
        idempotency_key: OMIT,
        request_options: nil
      )
        @client.request(
          :post,
          "/v3/phone/send/",
          body: {
            "phone_number" => phone_number,
            "options" => options,
            "signals" => signals,
            "vendor_data" => vendor_data,
            "metadata" => metadata,
            "code" => code,
            "code_length" => code_length,
            "on_conflict" => on_conflict,
            "trusted_user" => trusted_user,
            "force_challenge" => force_challenge,
            "fraud_override" => fraud_override
          },
          headers: { "Idempotency-Key" => idempotency_key },
          response: Types::PhoneVerificationSendResponse,
          request_options: request_options
        )
      end

      # Validate and format a phone number
      #
      # Check that a number can exist and return it formatted, without looking anything up: `valid`, `format.e164`,
      # `format.national` and `country`. Send the number in international format, or a national number with `country`.
      #
      # If the number parses but is invalid for its numbering plan, the response is `200` with `valid: false` and both
      # formats. Validation and formatting are free: `charged` is always `false`.
      #
      # **Errors.** `400 invalid_phone_number`: not a number at all.
      #
      # A scoped API key needs `phone: write`.
      #
      # +POST /v3/phone/validate/+
      #
      # @param phone_number [String] The number in international format, or a national number with `country`.
      # @param country [String] The ISO 3166-1 alpha-2 code of a national number.
      # @param idempotency_key [String] sent as the Idempotency-Key header
      # @param request_options [Hash, nil] :timeout, :max_retries, :extra_headers, :extra_query, :extra_body
      # @return [Didit::Types::PhoneValidateResponse]
      def validate(phone_number:, country: OMIT, idempotency_key: OMIT, request_options: nil)
        @client.request(
          :post,
          "/v3/phone/validate/",
          body: {
            "phone_number" => phone_number,
            "country" => country
          },
          headers: { "Idempotency-Key" => idempotency_key },
          response: Types::PhoneValidateResponse,
          request_options: request_options
        )
      end

